entries=[]
def add(id,title,summary,definitions,result,example,limits,extension,refs,status='Classical result developed and example checked'):
    entries.append(dict(id=id,title=title,status=status,summary=summary,definitions=definitions,result=result,example=example,limits=limits,extension=extension,references=[dict(label=a,url=b) for a,b in refs]))

add('M100','Bernstein–Sato certificates and a minimality proof',
    'The original broad application question becomes an exact operator identity with a lower-bound argument. A certificate proves that a polynomial works; minimality proves that it is the b-function. Both steps can be completed for monomials, and a linear coordinate change supplies a nonmonomial example.',
    ['Work over C in the Weyl algebra generated by x and ∂ with ∂x−x∂=1. The parameter s commutes with both generators.',
     'A Bernstein–Sato relation is P(s)f^(s+1)=b(s)f^s in the formal localization module. The global b-function is the monic polynomial of least degree satisfying such a relation.'],
    dict(name='Exact monomial b-function',statement='For m≥1, b_(x^m)(s)=∏_(j=1)^m(s+j/m), with certificate P(s)=m^(−m)∂^m.',
         proof='Differentiate x^(m(s+1)) exactly m times. The coefficient is ∏_(k=0)^(m−1)(m(s+1)−k)=m^m∏_(j=1)^m(s+j/m), proving the certificate. To prove minimality, put an arbitrary polynomial-coefficient operator in normal order Σ a_ij(s)x^i∂^j. Its action contributes the Laurent monomial x^(ms+m+i−j). The coefficient of x^(ms) can only receive terms with j=m+i. Every such falling-factorial coefficient contains all m factors from the displayed certificate. The other Laurent powers are independent over C(s), so any possible b(s) is divisible by that monic product. Thus the candidate is minimal, not merely an annihilating multiple.'),
    dict(setup='Take f=x²y³ and then the nonmonomial f=x²+y².',calculation='For x²y³, P=(1/108)∂ₓ²∂ᵧ³ gives b(s)=(s+1/2)(s+1/3)(s+2/3)(s+1)². The same exponent-comparison argument in both variables proves minimality. Over C set u=x+iy and v=x−iy. Then x²+y²=uv and ∂ᵤ∂ᵥ=(1/4)(∂ₓ²+∂ᵧ²), so (1/4)Δf^(s+1)=(s+1)²f^s.',conclusion='A polynomial automorphism preserves the defining differential-operator problem, so the nonmonomial quadratic has b(s)=(s+1)². Repeated roots carry actual multiplicity; they must not be discarded.'),
    'These are global polynomial examples in characteristic zero. General singularities may require local rather than global b-functions, elimination in a Weyl algebra, or additional singularity theory. The derivation does not establish that every proposed physical or statistical application admits a useful b-function.',
    'A worthwhile next calculation is to compute a local b-function for a chosen non-normal-crossing plane singularity, retaining the operator certificate and a separate minimality argument. An unverified candidate formula should be labeled as such.',
    [('Álvarez Montaner and coauthors Bernstein–Sato functional equations','https://web.mat.upc.edu/josep.alvarez/pdf/Vfiltration.pdf'),('Saito Introduction to a theory of b-functions','https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0610783')])

add('M101','Conic polarity as a typed point-to-line map',
    'The corrected construction identifies the quadratic form first, then derives polarity, the dual conic and coordinate covariance. It removes the undefined polar-line formula and the invented idea that one point dualizes to a conic.',
    ['Let Q be an invertible symmetric 3×3 matrix over a field of characteristic different from two. Points are nonzero column vectors modulo scale; lines are nonzero covectors modulo scale.',
     'The nonsingular conic is C={x:xᵀQx=0}. The polar line of p has covector ℓ=Qp, because its equation is pᵀQx=0.'],
    dict(name='Polarity and the dual quadratic form',statement='The polarity p↦Qp is invertible, incidence is reciprocal, and the tangent-line locus in the dual projective plane is ℓᵀQ⁻¹ℓ=0.',
         proof='At a conic point a, differentiating xᵀQx gives tangent covector 2Qa, which defines the same projective line as Qa. Conversely any ℓ has pole p=Q⁻¹ℓ. That line is tangent precisely when its pole lies on C, which is exactly ℓᵀQ⁻¹ℓ=0. Reciprocity follows from pᵀQq=qᵀQp. Under x′=Mx, Q′=M⁻ᵀQM⁻¹ and ℓ′=M⁻ᵀℓ; hence Q′p′=M⁻ᵀQp. The construction therefore respects projective coordinate changes rather than depending on one affine drawing.'),
    dict(setup='Use the real unit-circle conic x²+y²−z²=0 and p=(2,0,1).',calculation='Here Q=diag(1,1,−1), so the polar is 2x−z=0. Its intersections with the conic are a±=(1/2,±√3/2,1). The tangents at these points have covectors (1/2,±√3/2,−1), and each contains p because their pairing with p is zero. The polar itself is not tangent: its dual quadratic value is 2²−1²=3.',conclusion='The polar joins the two contact points of tangents drawn from the exterior point. Applying Q⁻¹ to its covector returns p. This verifies the point-line correspondence and the dual-conic test separately.'),
    'Invertibility of Q and the characteristic assumption matter. A degenerate quadratic form does not produce a global polarity. Real exterior/interior terminology has no direct analogue over every field; projective incidence survives, while real tangent counts may change.',
    'For higher-dimensional quadrics the same matrix argument defines polar hyperplanes. A useful further problem is to classify how the construction degenerates as det Q tends to zero and which incidence statements remain valid.',
    [])

add('M102','Cross-ratio with a fixed ordering and homogeneous coordinates',
    'Cross-ratio becomes a precise projective invariant once the four-point order and convention are fixed. Harmonicity is then a concrete value of that invariant, not an order-free label.',
    ['For distinct projective points a,b,c,d on P¹, choose nonzero representatives v_a,…,v_d and write [u,v]=det(u,v).',
     'Use CR(a,b;c,d)=[v_a,v_c][v_b,v_d]/([v_b,v_c][v_a,v_d]). In an affine chart this is ((c−a)(d−b))/((c−b)(d−a)).'],
    dict(name='Projective invariance and harmonic ordering',statement='Every invertible 2×2 matrix preserves this ordered cross-ratio. Under permutations the possible values lie in {r,1/r,1−r,1/(1−r),r/(r−1),(r−1)/r}.',
         proof='Rescaling any representative multiplies one numerator factor and one denominator factor by the same scalar. Applying M multiplies each bracket by det M; the two determinant factors above and below cancel. Thus CR is both projectively well defined and invariant. The permutation formulas follow by swapping arguments in the affine expression. In particular r=−1 can become 2 or 1/2 after a different ordering, so a harmonic assertion must retain its convention. Homogeneous representatives make the point at infinity routine rather than an exception requiring an invented formula.'),
    dict(setup='Take (a,b;c,d)=(−1,1;0,∞) and T(z)=(2z+3)/(z+2).',calculation='Using v∞=(1,0) gives CR=−1. The transformation has nonzero determinant one and sends the ordered tuple to (1,5/3;3/2,2). The transformed ratio is [(1/2)(1/3)]/[(-1/6)(1)]=−1, verified with rational arithmetic.',conclusion='The example simultaneously checks infinity, ordering and projective invariance. A dual statement for four concurrent lines follows only after identifying their pencil with P¹; duality is not a license to replace undefined point coordinates by arbitrary line expressions.'),
    'The four points must be distinct for the displayed nondegenerate ratio. Coinciding points require a limiting or compactified configuration-space interpretation. Over a general field, the listed six values can coincide more than they do over the real numbers.',
    'Cross-ratio coordinates give a concrete entry into moduli of marked projective lines: normalize three points to 0,1,∞ and the fourth is the remaining parameter. Investigate degenerations by tracking this parameter toward 0,1 or ∞.',
    [])

add('M103','A Heisenberg field and its verified local algebra',
    'A worked Fock-space model supplies actual operators, a vacuum and a singular operator product. It shows what the abstract vertex-algebra vocabulary is organizing, without claiming that a few words or one OPE alone constitute the full theory.',
    ['Let F=C[x₁,x₂,…], with each polynomial involving finitely many variables. Set a₋ₙ=xₙ by multiplication and aₙ=n∂/∂xₙ for n>0; let a₀ act as zero.',
     'The vacuum is 1. The current is J(z)=Σ_(n∈Z)aₙz^(−n−1), interpreted as a formal field. Normal ordering moves creation modes a₋ₙ to the left.'],
    dict(name='Fock commutator and current contraction',statement='[a_m,a_n]=mδ_(m+n,0)I. The singular contraction of J(z)J(w) is 1/(z−w)², in the formal expansion associated with |z|>|w|.',
         proof='For positive m,n, derivatives commute and multiplications commute. The only nonzero mixed commutator is [m∂ₓₘ,xₙ]=mδ_mn. Splitting the field into annihilation and creation parts gives [J₊(z),J₋(w)]=Σ_(n≥1)n z^(−n−1)w^(n−1). Differentiating the formal geometric series Σ_(n≥0)wⁿz^(−n−1)=1/(z−w) produces the claimed double pole. The normally ordered remainder has no additional singular contraction. This calculation is a complete local commutator derivation in a defined representation.'),
    dict(setup='Use the normally ordered stress tensor T(z)=(1/2):J(z)²: and vacuum states of low degree.',calculation='The mode L₋₂ sends 1 to x₁²/2. The only contribution of L₂ on this state is (1/2)a₁², giving L₂L₋₂1=1/2. With L₀1=0, the Virasoro convention [L₂,L₋₂]=4L₀+cI/2 yields c=1. Translation on generators is represented by T₀=Σ_(n≥1)n xₙ₊₁∂ₓₙ, so [T₀,a₋ₙ]=n a₋₍ₙ₊₁₎.',conclusion='The example fixes normalization of the central term and makes the relation between formal fields and modes explicit. The verifier checks the displayed operator relations on a finite set of polynomial states.'),
    'The finite checks do not prove all vertex-algebra axioms. The complete Heisenberg vertex algebra follows from the usual reconstruction construction, with vacuum, translation and locality stated on the full formal space. A coordinate-free chiral-algebra construction additionally needs a curve and coordinate transformation laws.',
    'Develop conformal blocks for this explicit algebra on a chosen curve, or prove a stated reconstruction theorem. Such a continuation has concrete input data rather than a generic appeal to string theory.',
    [('Frenkel and Ben-Zvi Vertex Algebras and Algebraic Curves','https://math.berkeley.edu/~frenkel/BOOK/')])

add('M104','Four-site XXX spectrum and the regular one-magnon roots',
    'The corrected Bethe equations are tied to one Hamiltonian normalization and checked against a finite matrix. The resulting calculation verifies a sector, while keeping singular-root and completeness questions explicit.',
    ['Take N=4 periodic spin-1/2 sites and H=Σ_(j=0)^3(I−P_(j,j+1)), where P swaps adjacent spins and the last edge wraps around.',
     'In the one-down-spin sector, |j⟩ denotes the down spin at site j. A plane wave has coefficients exp(ipj), with p=2πm/4.'],
    dict(name='Exact one-magnon diagonalization',statement='H|p⟩=(2−2cos p)|p⟩, so this four-dimensional sector has energies 0,2,4,2. The finite regular Bethe roots are λ=1/2,0,−1/2, giving energies 1/(λ²+1/4)=2,4,2.',
         proof='Only the two bonds incident on the down spin act nontrivially, giving H|j⟩=2|j⟩−|j−1⟩−|j+1⟩. Substitution of the plane wave yields the dispersion. In the periodic XXX convention the regular roots obey ((λ_j+i/2)/(λ_j−i/2))^N=∏_(k≠j)(λ_j−λ_k+i)/(λ_j−λ_k−i). For one root the right side is one. Writing exp(ip)=(λ+i/2)/(λ−i/2) gives λ=(1/2)cot(p/2), and the energy agrees with the plane-wave dispersion. The p=0 state corresponds to the infinite-root limit.'),
    dict(setup='Use real vectors in the ordered basis |0⟩,…,|3⟩.',calculation='The vectors (1,1,1,1), (1,0,−1,0), (0,1,0,−1) and (1,−1,1,−1) form a basis with eigenvalues 0,2,2,4. Exact integer matrix multiplication verifies all four. For λ=1/2,0,−1/2 the complex Bethe ratio is i,−1,−i respectively; its fourth power is one.',conclusion='This establishes agreement between a direct operator calculation and the regular Bethe description of the nonuniform one-magnon states. It also demonstrates why omitting the infinite-root state would lose an actual eigenvector.'),
    'The calculation does not prove completeness in other magnetization sectors or for arbitrary N. Roots at ±i/2 and coincident roots need a regularization or a carefully specified admissibility convention. Including the self-index k=j would add a spurious factor and changes the equations.',
    'For the two-magnon sector, diagonalize the six-dimensional matrix first, then match admissible Bethe solutions and explicitly account for singular solutions and descendants. The matrix spectrum supplies an independent target.',
    [('Faddeev How Algebraic Bethe Ansatz works for integrable model','https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9605187')])

add('M105','A complete two-site Gaudin to scalar-oper calculation',
    'A concrete spectral bridge replaces the unsupported Fourier-to-automorphic-form slogan. In the smallest nontrivial sl₂ example, the Bethe vector, Hamiltonian eigenvalues, Miura potential and cancellation of the apparent singularity can all be computed exactly.',
    ['On each C² take h=diag(1,−1), e|↓⟩=|↑⟩, f|↑⟩=|↓⟩ and Ω=(1/2)h⊗h+e⊗f+f⊗e. Choose marked points z₁=0,z₂=1.',
     'Let H₁=Ω/(z₁−z₂)=−Ω and H₂=Ω. For weights (1,1), one Bethe root obeys 1/w+1/(w−1)=0.'],
    dict(name='Singlet spectral data and apparent-pole cancellation',statement='The root w=1/2 yields a singlet with (H₁,H₂) eigenvalues (3/2,−3/2). Define u(x)=1/(2x)+1/(2(x−1))−1/(x−1/2). Then (∂−u)(∂+u)=∂²−V, where V=u²−u′=3/[4x²(x−1)²].',
         proof='The Bethe vector is w⁻¹|↓↑⟩+(w−1)⁻¹|↑↓⟩=2|↓↑⟩−2|↑↓⟩. Direct use of Ω gives eigenvalue −3/2, hence the Hamiltonian pair above. Near a root w, write u=−1/(x−w)+a₀+O(x−w). The double pole cancels in u²−u′, and its simple residue is −2a₀. Here a₀=(1/2)(1/w+1/(w−1))=0 precisely by the Bethe equation. Expanding at the physical points gives V=3/(4x²)+3/(4(x−1)²)+(3/2)/x−(3/2)/(x−1). Its simple residues match H₁ and H₂ in the declared normalization.'),
    dict(setup='Treat the scalar differential equation y″−V(x)y=0 on the punctured projective line.',calculation='The apparent point x=1/2 is absent from V. One solution is y₁=(x−1/2)/sqrt(x(x−1)). Reduction of order gives y₂=(x²−x/2+1/4)/sqrt(x(x−1)), with Wronskian one. Both are regular at x=1/2. Around x=0 or x=1 their common square-root factor changes sign, so monodromy is scalar and projectively trivial. The vacuum sector, with no root, instead has simple residues (−1/2,1/2), matching the triplet eigenvalues.',conclusion='The calculation supplies an actual rank-one instance of the Gaudin–oper relationship: eigenvalues are encoded in coefficients of a scalar differential operator. The simple finite example is classical; its explicit replay is an exposition and verification result.'),
    'This is an sl₂ example, not a proof of the full Gaudin-algebra spectrum theorem, a global automorphic correspondence or arbitrary Bethe completeness. Oper terminology and Langlands duality must retain the relevant group, singularity and monodromy conditions.',
    'Move to three marked spin-1/2 sites, compute each highest-weight multiplicity space, and test how coalescing sites affect the Bethe roots and oper singularities. This gives a controlled extension with independent matrix checks.',
    [('Frenkel Gaudin model and opers','https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0407524')])

# Carry the three-site extension through to a complete finite calculation.
g105=entries[-1]
g105['summary']+=' A rational three-site continuation also gives a complete eight-dimensional joint eigenbasis and two additional scalar-oper certificates.'
g105['example']['calculation']+=' For sites (0,1,8/3), the one-root equation reduces to 3w²−(22/3)w+8/3=0, with roots 2 and 4/9. Their eigenvalue triples are (−3/16,6/5,−81/80) and (25/16,−8/5,3/80). The highest vectors have one-down-spin coefficients (1/2,1,−3/2) and (9/4,−9/5,−9/20). Applying total lowering once to each gives two doublets; applying it zero through three times to the vacuum gives a quartet with eigenvalues (−11/16,1/5,39/80). All eight rational vectors are independent and satisfy all three full 8×8 Hamiltonians. For each regular root, with g=∏(x−z_i), u=g′/(2g)−1/(x−w) gives V=(3/4)Σ_i(x−z_i)^(−2)+Σ_iE_i/(x−z_i). Exact residual polynomials vanish. Since g′(w)=0, reduction of order has no logarithm: y₁=(x−w)/sqrt(g), y₂=[−g(w)+(g″(w)/2)(x−w)²+(x−w)³/2]/sqrt(g). Their Wronskian is one, and both ODE residuals vanish identically.'
g105['example']['conclusion']+=' The three-site continuation settles this complete finite joint-spectrum example, including its scalar equations, rather than leaving it as a proposed computation.'
g105['extension']='S010 develops the arbitrary-site one-magnon theorem, S013 completes the three-site exceptional-point analysis, and S014 classifies every distinct-complex-site one-magnon Jordan block with closed matrix and characteristic formulas. Remaining extensions concern multi-magnon sectors, higher representations or physical-site collisions; they require additional hypotheses and independent checks. This is separate from full Gaudin completeness.'

add('M106','Joint reliability is a dependence problem',
    'The arithmetic correction is only the first step. An all-success probability cannot be identified from marginal accuracies alone. Exact dependence constructions show the full gap between what the individual probabilities say and what independence adds.',
    ['Let A₁,…,A_n be success events with P(A_i)=p_i. The target is P(∩A_i), a joint probability.',
     'Mutual independence means P(∩_(i∈S)A_i)=∏_(i∈S)p_i for every subset S. Pairwise independence checks only subsets of size two.'],
    dict(name='Fréchet bounds and an independence counterexample',statement='max(0,Σp_i−(n−1))≤P(∩A_i)≤min_i p_i. If all 104 marginals are 0.95, these bounds are 0 and 0.95, whereas independence gives 0.95^104≈0.00482230805327.',
         proof='The upper bound follows because the intersection is a subset of every A_i. For the lower bound apply the union bound to the failure events: P(∩A_i)=1−P(∪A_iᶜ)≥1−Σ(1−p_i). Probabilities are also nonnegative. The product formula follows only after imposing mutual independence. To see why pairwise independence is insufficient, let X,Y be independent fair bits and set Z=X xor Y. Any pair is independent, but P(X=Y=Z=1)=0 rather than 1/8.'),
    dict(setup='Construct joint laws for 104 events, each with success probability 0.95.',calculation='For the maximal case take every A_i={U≤0.95} for one uniform U; then all-success probability is 0.95. For a zero case let failures be arcs of length 0.05 on the unit circle, starting at i/104. Since adjacent starts are less than 0.05 apart, the arcs cover the circle, so at least one failure always occurs, although each event still succeeds with probability 0.95. Independent Bernoulli variables give the intermediate product.',conclusion='The same individual accuracy is compatible with no chance of complete correctness, a 95 percent chance, or the independent-model value. Replacing a dependency model by repeated multiplication can therefore be a major modeling error even after arithmetic is repaired.'),
    'The numerical examples are abstract probability constructions, not estimated properties of a genealogy or any real audit. Real dependence requires observations or structural assumptions. Pairwise correlations alone generally do not determine the required high-order intersection.',
    'For a specified dependency graph, derive an interval using available joint moments or solve a finite linear program over atomic outcomes. Report the identified interval if the joint probability remains underdetermined.',
    [])

add('M107','Nonlinearity does not imply chaos or identifiability',
    'A rigorous counterexample is more informative than another catalogue of nonlinear applications. A simple nonlinear ODE has a fully solvable, globally convergent flow and an observation that permanently hides the sign of its initial state.',
    ['Consider ẋ=−x³ on the real line for t≥0. The state is x, and first choose the observable y=x².',
     'An observation model is identifiable on a set if no two admissible initial states generate exactly the same observed time history. This is distinct from state stability.'],
    dict(name='Global convergence with an exact observation collision',statement='For every initial x₀, x(t)=x₀/sqrt(1+2x₀²t). All trajectories converge to zero, while x₀ and −x₀ give identical y(t)=x₀²/(1+2x₀²t).',
         proof='For x₀≠0, differentiate x⁻² to obtain d(x⁻²)/dt=2 and solve; x₀=0 gives the equilibrium. The denominator stays positive, giving a global forward solution. With V=x²/2, V̇=−x⁴≤0, and the explicit solution proves convergence. Squaring removes the initial sign for all times. Equivalently y satisfies ẏ=−2y² with the same initial y₀ for both states. Thus the nonlinear system is elementary and convergent, while the chosen observation remains structurally ambiguous.'),
    dict(setup='Compare x₀=1 and x₀=−1, recording y=x² at any finite or infinite collection of times.',calculation='At t=0,1,2 the common observations are 1,1/3,1/5. No increase in temporal sampling resolves the sign because the entire continuous histories coincide. Adding the observable z=x distinguishes the two immediately. The exact rational y values and the observation evolution equation are checked by the verifier.',conclusion='Complexity of the evolution law, richness of sampling and distinguishability of states are separate questions. This example refutes a generic inference from nonlinear equations to chaos and a generic inference from many measurements to complete state reconstruction.'),
    'This proof concerns one scalar system and one observation. It is not a general criterion for absence of chaos in higher dimensions. Nor does adding a sign-sensitive observation establish practical robustness when measurement noise is large.',
    'Classify observation collisions under a symmetry group: if both dynamics and output are sign-equivariant or sign-invariant in the appropriate way, determine whether the output can at best recover a symmetry orbit. Then quantify the additional measurement needed to break it.',
    [])

add('M108','The Klein–Gordon dispersion relation and its massless limit',
    'The mathematical comparison starts with two explicitly normalized differential operators. Their plane-wave dispersions, propagation equations and interpretation can then be compared without confusing a classical field equation with the procedure of quantization.',
    ['Use coordinates (t,x), speed c and □=c⁻²∂_t²−Δ. The free Klein–Gordon equation is (□+(mc/ℏ)²)φ=0.',
     'A plane wave is exp(i(k·x−ωt)). Its wave vector k has inverse-length units, while ω has inverse-time units.'],
    dict(name='Massive and massless plane-wave dispersions',statement='Substitution gives ω²=c²|k|²+m²c⁴/ℏ². Setting m=0 gives the classical massless wave dispersion ω²=c²|k|².',
         proof='The second time derivative multiplies the wave by −ω² and the Laplacian by −|k|². The PDE therefore reduces to −ω²/c²+|k|²+(mc/ℏ)²=0. Multiplying by ℏ² and writing E=ℏω and p=ℏk gives E²=c²|p|²+m²c⁴. For m>0, differentiating the positive branch gives group velocity v_g=c²k/ω with magnitude below c; at high frequency it approaches c. These are consequences of the chosen PDE and Fourier ansatz, not additional quantum postulates.'),
    dict(setup='In units c=ℏ=1 take m=2 and spatial mode k=(3,0,0).',calculation='The frequencies are ω=±sqrt(13), and each wave has zero PDE residual because −13+9+4=0. The positive-branch group speed is 3/sqrt(13). On a periodic box of side L, allowed wave vectors are 2πn/L, producing a countable family of uncoupled harmonic oscillators at the classical level.',conclusion='Mass changes the relation between frequency and wave number. The periodic Fourier decomposition also identifies the finite-mode truncations that can be computed exactly; it does not by itself choose a Fock space or turn amplitudes into operators.'),
    'Negative-frequency solutions do not by themselves establish a negative-probability theory. Quantization and the choice of conserved inner product require their own construction. Interactions, curved backgrounds and boundary conditions change the analysis.',
    'For a specified potential or curved metric, determine whether a positive self-adjoint spatial operator supplies a stable mode expansion. Retain the operator domain and boundary conditions rather than transferring the flat-space dispersion unchanged.',
    [('Tong Free Fields lecture chapter','https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S2.html')])

add('M109','A controlled formal route from Dirac to Pauli',
    'The nonrelativistic limit must preserve spin and the magnetic coupling. Removing the rest-energy phase and eliminating the small spinor component gives the leading Pauli equation, together with an explicit statement of the scale assumptions.',
    ['Let π=p−qA with p=−iℏ∇ and B=∇×A. Use H_D=cα·π+βmc²+qΦ and a four-spinor ψ=e^(−imc²t/ℏ)(ϕ,χ)ᵀ.',
     'The nonrelativistic regime requires typical |π|/(mc)≪1 and energies and time variation small compared with mc², together with fields smooth enough for the expansion.'],
    dict(name='Leading spin-sensitive Hamiltonian',statement='At leading order χ≈(σ·π)ϕ/(2mc), and iℏϕ̇=[π²/(2m)−(qℏ/(2m))σ·B+qΦ]ϕ.',
         proof='After extracting the rest-energy phase, the two components satisfy iℏϕ̇=qΦϕ+cσ·πχ and iℏχ̇=(qΦ−2mc²)χ+cσ·πϕ. The large 2mc² denominator permits the stated leading elimination of χ when the remaining terms are small. Pauli-matrix multiplication gives (σ·π)²=π²+(i/2)Σε_ijkσ_k[π_i,π_j]. Since [π_i,π_j]=iqℏε_ijkB_k, the second term is −qℏσ·B. Inserting this identity into the first equation produces the Hamiltonian and fixes the sign convention.'),
    dict(setup='First take A=Φ=0 and a plane wave of momentum magnitude p.',calculation='The exact positive relativistic energy is sqrt(m²c⁴+c²p²)=mc²+p²/(2m)−p⁴/(8m³c²)+O(p⁶/(m⁵c⁴)). Removing mc² leaves the Schrödinger kinetic term at leading order. For a magnetic field, the additional Pauli spin term survives at that same effective order; a scalar wave equation would miss it.',conclusion='The result specifies both the limiting equation and the leading neglected kinetic correction. Its small parameter is p/(mc), not simply the statement that an experiment looks classical.'),
    'This is a formal asymptotic derivation for a prescribed background field. A uniform error theorem over long times needs operator estimates and additional regularity. Strong fields and pair-production regimes invalidate the elementary elimination.',
    'Choose a bounded momentum sector and smooth bounded fields, then derive a finite-time norm estimate comparing projected Dirac dynamics with the effective Pauli evolution. That is the appropriate next step toward a controlled approximation theorem.',
    [('Tong Spinor Fields lecture chapter','https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S4.html')])

add('M110','Maxwell equations as field equations and identities',
    'Differential forms separate what follows from a potential from what must be imposed as a sourced equation. Gauge redundancy becomes a precise kernel of the map from potentials to field strength, with topology supplying an important qualification.',
    ['On an oriented spacetime with a fixed metric, A is a one-form and F=dA is a two-form. The Hodge star depends on that metric and orientation.',
     'Use normalization dF=0 and d⋆F=J, where J is a source three-form. Constants can be restored according to electromagnetic unit conventions.'],
    dict(name='Gauge invariance and current closure',statement='A↦A+dχ leaves F unchanged. Whenever d⋆F=J holds, dJ=0. Locally, every closed F is a potential field on a contractible coordinate patch.',
         proof='The identity d²=0 gives d(A+dχ)=dA. Applying d to the sourced equation gives dJ=d²⋆F=0, so source conservation is a compatibility condition. The local converse dF=0⇒F=dA is the Poincaré lemma under the contractibility assumption. It is not a global theorem on arbitrary manifolds: nontrivial degree-two cohomology can obstruct a single globally defined potential. The coordinate form of dF=0 is the homogeneous Maxwell system; the Hodge-star equation contains the metric-dependent dynamical content.'),
    dict(setup='On flat coordinates (t,x,y,z), choose A=a cos(kx−ωt)dy and use units c=1.',calculation='Then F=−ak sin(kx−ωt)dx∧dy+aω sin(kx−ωt)dt∧dy. Direct exterior differentiation gives dF=0 because the two mixed terms cancel. The vacuum sourced equation requires ω²=k². Adding dχ for χ=λtx changes components of A but contributes no change to F.',conclusion='The example distinguishes the automatic homogeneous identity, the dispersion imposed by the vacuum field equation and a gauge change of the potential. It also shows why covariant notation alone is not a quantization procedure.'),
    'Global potentials, boundary conditions and gauge fixing require separate analysis. A flat connection can have nontrivial global holonomy while being locally pure gauge. A globally exact potential A=dχ with single-valued χ has no such loop holonomy; these cases must not be conflated.',
    'Develop the same problem on a torus or a punctured domain and calculate closed forms that are not exact. This makes the topology of gauge data explicit before introducing a quantum theory.',
    [('Tong Gauge Theory electromagnetism notes','https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/gaugetheory/1em.pdf')])

add('M111','Ward–Takahashi constrains a vertex without determining it',
    'The identity is made concrete by verifying it at tree level, constructing a longitudinal vertex for a prescribed inverse propagator, and displaying the remaining transverse freedom. This explains both the power and the limitation of the constraint.',
    ['Factor the charge from the full electromagnetic vertex Γ^μ. With consistent momentum assignments, the identity is q_μΓ^μ(p+q,p)=S⁻¹(p+q)−S⁻¹(p).',
     'A transverse addition T^μ satisfies q_μT^μ=0. Such an addition cannot be detected by this contracted identity alone.'],
    dict(name='An explicit longitudinal construction',statement='For a differentiable matrix-valued inverse propagator K(p)=S⁻¹(p), Γ_L^μ=∫₀¹(∂K/∂p_μ)(p+tq)dt satisfies the difference identity on any regular path. It is not the unique satisfying vertex.',
         proof='Contract with q and use the chain rule: q_μ∂K(p+tq)/∂p_μ=dK(p+tq)/dt. The fundamental theorem of calculus gives K(p+q)−K(p). Adding any transverse T preserves the contraction. At tree level K(p)=slash(p)−m and Γ^μ=γ^μ, reducing the identity to slash(q)=slash(p+q)−slash(p). These are direct algebraic checks of the kinematic relation; they do not assert that an arbitrarily chosen K and Γ arise from a complete interacting quantum field theory.'),
    dict(setup='For an illustrative polynomial matrix function, take K(p)=slash(p)−m−αp²I.',calculation='The construction gives Γ_L^μ=γ^μ−α(2p^μ+q^μ)I. Contracting yields slash(q)−α(2p·q+q²)I, exactly K(p+q)−K(p). One may add κ(q²p^μ−(p·q)q^μ)I, whose contraction is zero. The verifier checks this example in a two-coordinate Euclidean Clifford representation with rational inputs.',conclusion='The identity constrains differences of inverse propagators and the longitudinal vertex. It leaves dynamical transverse information undetermined. The polynomial example tests the logic without making a false renormalization claim.'),
    'In QED the full identity is a statement about regularized and renormalized correlation functions. A symmetry-breaking regulator, inconsistent counterterms or an anomalous symmetry can require additional terms or restoration conditions. The algebraic construction above does not settle those issues.',
    'Specify an actual perturbative action and regulator, calculate the one-loop self-energy and vertex in the same convention, and check the renormalized identity including counterterms. Retain transverse form factors as independent output.',
    [('Beisert Quantum Field Theory II notes','https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/QFT2-17FS-Notes.pdf')])

add('M112','A complete finite-dimensional perturbation calculation',
    'A zero-dimensional quartic model permits an exact derivation of propagator contractions, vacuum normalization, connected coefficients and a rigorous asymptotic remainder. It supplies a fully checkable model of perturbative logic without claiming to construct a continuum field theory.',
    ['Let X be a real standard Gaussian and define Z(g)=E[exp(−gX⁴/24)] for g≥0. The normalized two-point function is G₂(g)=E[X²exp(−gX⁴/24)]/Z(g).',
     'Gaussian moments are E[X^(2r)]=(2r−1)!! and odd moments vanish. Wick pairings count those double factorials.'],
    dict(name='Quartic coefficients and a controlled remainder',statement='Z(g)=1−g/8+35g²/384+O(g³), log Z(g)=−g/8+g²/12+O(g³), and G₂(g)=1−g/2+2g²/3+O(g³) as g↓0.',
         proof='Expand the interaction exponential to a fixed order and integrate each Gaussian moment. The nth coefficient of Z is a_n=(−1)^n(4n−1)!!/(24^n n!). Multiplying and dividing the resulting formal series gives the displayed connected and normalized coefficients. For g≥0, Taylor’s integral remainder for exp(−y) has magnitude at most y^(N+1)/(N+1)!; taking y=gX⁴/24 and integrating bounds the partition-function remainder by the next absolute coefficient times g^(N+1). This proves a finite-order asymptotic statement without exchanging an infinite series and the integral.'),
    dict(setup='Calculate through second order using integer Gaussian moments.',calculation='E[X⁴]=3, E[X⁸]=105 and E[X¹⁰]=945. Thus the numerator of G₂ is 1−5g/8+105g²/128+O(g³); division by Z gives 1−g/2+2g²/3. Meanwhile |a_(n+1)/a_n|=(4n+3)(4n+1)/(24(n+1)), which grows without bound.',conclusion='The perturbative series has zero radius of convergence although the original integral exists for every g≥0. Correct finite-order coefficients and an explicit asymptotic bound are therefore stronger and more honest deliverables than a claim that the infinite diagram sum converges.'),
    'This toy integral has no spacetime ultraviolet divergences, locality or scattering theory. It does not replace renormalization of a continuum model. Its value is the exact separation of algebraic combinatorics, normalization and asymptotic control.',
    'Add several Gaussian variables with a stated covariance and derive connected cumulants. For a continuum problem, introduce a cutoff and identify which bounds survive its removal rather than assuming the finite-dimensional argument is uniform.',
    [('Tong Interacting Fields lecture chapter','https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/qft/qfthtml/S3.html')])

add('M113','An exact RG toy flow and an invariant local exponent',
    'The beta function must be specified before discussing running couplings. Simple autonomous flows can be solved exactly, and the derivative at a fixed point gives a local quantity that survives smooth invertible reparametrization of the coupling.',
    ['Write t=log(μ/μ₀) and dg/dt=β(g). A fixed point g* satisfies β(g*)=0.',
     'A scheme coordinate change is a smooth locally invertible h=h(g). Its beta function is β_h(h(g))=h′(g)β(g). This is a coordinate statement for an exact flow, not a guarantee about truncated calculations. The nonzero-initial-condition formula below assumes g₀≠0; g₀=0 is the constant equilibrium.'],
    dict(name='Flow solutions and fixed-point linearization',statement='For β=bg², g(t)=g₀/(1−bg₀t). For β=−εg+bg² with ε,b>0 and g*=ε/b, g(t)=g*/[1+(g*/g₀−1)e^(εt)] wherever its denominator is nonzero. At a fixed point, dβ_h/dh=β′(g*).',
         proof='The first solution follows by differentiating 1/g. For the second, substitution or separation of variables verifies the displayed logistic-type formula. Linearization gives δġ=β′(g*)δg. Differentiating β_h=h′β with respect to h yields (h″β+h′β′)/h′. At a fixed point β=0, so the derivative equals β′. Thus the local exponent is invariant under the declared regular change of variable, although the numerical coordinate used to label the fixed point can change.'),
    dict(setup='Take ε=b=1 and g₀=1/2.',calculation='Then g(t)=1/(1+e^t). It approaches the Gaussian fixed point zero as μ increases and the nonzero fixed point one as μ decreases. The derivatives are β′(0)=−1 and β′(1)=1, giving the stated ultraviolet and infrared stability directions. For β=g² with g₀=1/2, the toy solution has a pole at t=2.',conclusion='These statements are exact for the selected differential equations. A pole in a one-loop approximation does not prove that an underlying physical theory has an actual singularity there; it marks where the approximation can no longer justify extrapolation.'),
    'A field-theoretic beta function depends on the specified action, regulator, renormalization prescription and perturbative order. Multiple couplings require a Jacobian matrix, and marginal directions require nonlinear analysis. An arbitrary scalar cost function is not automatically an RG flow.',
    'For a specified two-coupling beta vector field, solve the fixed-point equations, classify its Jacobian and compare the invariant eigenvalues under an explicit analytic coordinate change. Report the truncation order with every numerical prediction.',
    [('Beisert Quantum Field Theory II notes renormalization sections','https://people.phys.ethz.ch/~nbeisert/lectures/QFT2-17FS-Notes.pdf')])

add('M114','Moving level sets and the singularity of threshold coordinates',
    'An implicit boundary can be differentiated only where its defining level is regular. The same calculation quantifies sensitivity and gives an exact example where a boundary is born and its graph velocity diverges.',
    ['Let f(x,t) be continuously differentiable and consider the level set f(x,t)=c. A regular spatial level has ∇ₓf≠0.',
     'For a one-dimensional graph r(t), the condition is f_r(r(t),t)≠0. Choose the unit normal n=∇f/|∇f| when discussing normal velocity.'],
    dict(name='Implicit velocity and perturbation sensitivity',statement='Along a regular graph r′=−f_t/f_r. In several dimensions a moving level set has normal velocity v_n=−f_t/|∇f|. A small additive level perturbation δf produces first-order normal displacement −δf/|∇f|.',
         proof='Differentiate f(r(t),t)=c to obtain f_r r′+f_t=0. For a path X(t) on a spatial level, ∇f·X′+f_t=0; taking the normal component gives the velocity. For the perturbation, Taylor-expand f(X+δr n,t)+δf=c and use ∇f·n=|∇f|. The denominator therefore measures conditioning as well as existence of the local graph. Tangential motion is a parametrization choice and is not fixed by the level equation.'),
    dict(setup='Take f(x,y,t)=x²+y²−t and c=0.',calculation='For t>0 the boundary is a circle of radius sqrt(t), and its outward normal speed is 1/(2sqrt(t)). At t=0 the spatial gradient vanishes at the origin. For t<0 there is no real level set, at zero there is one point, and for t>0 there is a circle. The one-dimensional version r²−t=0 has two branches ±sqrt(t), neither a differentiable graph through the critical event.',conclusion='The diverging speed is not a contradiction in the implicit-function theorem; it occurs exactly where its nonzero-gradient hypothesis fails. The same inverse-gradient factor explains why threshold localization becomes unstable near a flat or critical level.'),
    'These are local regularity and first-order sensitivity statements. They do not guarantee a global graph, a unique topology, or robustness under large perturbations. Nonsmooth defining functions need generalized notions of normal and solution.',
    'For a specified smooth family, locate critical levels by solving f=c and ∇f=0 simultaneously, classify the local Hessian, and track which topology changes are compatible with those critical events.',
    [])

assert {e['id'] for e in entries}=={f'M{i:03}' for i in range(100,115)}
for e in entries:
    e['word_count']=len(' '.join([e['summary'],*e['definitions'],*e['result'].values(),*e['example'].values(),e['limits'],e['extension']]).split())
BASE.joinpath('entries.json').write_text(json.dumps(entries,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
print(json.dumps({'entries':len(entries),'words':sum(e['word_count'] for e in entries),'min_words':min(e['word_count'] for e in entries)}))
